{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"An in-kind ETF can meet large redemptions by delivering a basket of stocks to an authorized participant rather than selling stocks for cash. Under the RIC rules, a qualifying in-kind redemption generally does not make the fund recognize gain on appreciated securities distributed; the fund can select low-basis lots, reducing embedded gains. A cash-dealing mutual fund more often sells holdings to fund redemptions, realizing gains that may be distributed to shareholders and taxed in the year received.\n\nThis is a deferral/reduction of fund-level realized gains—not a guarantee of zero capital-gain distributions. It does not eliminate taxable dividends, gains the fund realizes in sales or portfolio changes, or tax when an investor sells ETF shares at a gain. Benefits vary with flows, turnover, gains, and the ETF’s ability to redeem in kind; investors can also owe tax on distributions even if they reinvest them. The mutual fund may likewise avoid sales when it has cash or offsetting losses."}}
